            <ArticleTitle>Inadvertant balloon extrusion through a side hole in a guiding catheter.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>23-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A patient presented with an acute inferior myocardial infarction. Coronary angiography in the acute stage revealed total occlusion of the right coronary artery. Reperfusion was obtained after intracoronary infusion of 250,000 units of streptokinase. Angioplasty was subsequently performed because of a high grade residual stenosis. An 8-French right Judkins guiding catheter with a single side hole (USCI), a 3.0 mm balloon dilatation catheter (ACS), and a 0.018 high torque floppy guide wire (ACS) were used. After successful angioplasty angiography was repeated with the guide wire in the RCA, but the balloon was withdrawn into the guiding catheter. After injection of contrast, it was impossible to withdrawn the balloon catheter out of the guiding catheter. Fluoroscopy revealed extrusion of the balloon through the side hole in the guiding catheter.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
